==== TEST 38389: hardware ACL (permit + deny) on a MASTER member port ====
started 2026-08-18T09:58:41+12:00
bench   : tb470, AT-IE520-28GSX stack, chassis-id 3439, VMAC 0000.cd37.0d6f
          software IE520-tb470.rel tomahawk_ie520-continuous, Build Fri Aug 14 2026
          member 1 = 264A23066 / 84e3.2787.0740 / console /dev/u5
          member 2 = 264A23052 / 84e3.2787.09c0 / console /dev/u4
target  : MASTER = stack member 2, ingress port port2.0.1
path    : tb470 eth2  ->  port2.0.1   (direct, isolated from the shared lab segment)
dest    : 10.38.215.34  (stack VLAN interface)
sources : 10.38.215.33  = expected PERMIT (explicit permit rule)
          10.38.215.35 = expected DENY   (explicit deny rule)
KEY SEMANTIC (from ck.db CLI ref, ipv4_hw_acl_cmd/accesslist_hardware_named_bi.html):
  Hardware ACLs will permit access unless explicitly denied by an ACL action.
  => the implicit action is PERMIT, not deny. A permit rule alone proves nothing, so
     this test measures the DENY rule and requires COUNTER HITS on BOTH rules as
     positive evidence rather than relying on 'traffic got through'.
  the ACL will only apply to incoming data packets  => ingress only.
NOTE: software ACLs cannot be applied to a port on this platform (access-group offers
      hardware lists only; 'ip access-group' is unrecognised on port and VLAN
      interfaces; 'access-class' unrecognised on line vty). Hardware ACL is the only
      option, consistent with the test title.

