######## STEP 2 RESULT — traffic follows the ACL rules ########
                                              expected   observed
  10.38.215.33 -> 10.38.215.34   (matches PERMIT)    pass       0% packet loss
  10.38.215.35 -> 10.38.215.34   (matches DENY)      blocked  100% packet loss
Compare against the PRE-ACL CONTROL above, where BOTH sources reached the target at
0% loss. The only change is the ACL, so the 100% loss is the deny rule acting.

POSITIVE EVIDENCE — show access-list counters (4 echo requests sent per source):
  Name: acl-master
      Rule: permit icmp 10.38.215.33/32 10.38.215.34/32
      Packet Hits: 4
      Rule: deny icmp 10.38.215.35/32 10.38.215.34/32
      Packet Hits: 4
Both rules register EXACTLY the 4 packets sent. This matters because hardware ACLs
implicitly PERMIT: without a counter hit, "traffic got through" would be equally
consistent with the permit rule never matching at all. The hit count rules that out.

######## VERDICT: PASS ########
Traffic followed the ACL rules on the MASTER member's port. Both the explicit permit
and the explicit deny behaved as specified, each confirmed by rule counters.

CROSS-REFERENCE: run jointly with TEST 38387 (BACKUP member port) in the same
session, same stack, same software, same procedure. Both members produced IDENTICAL
results - hardware ACL enforcement shows no master/backup difference.
